[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Introduction to Automation Testing and Test Pyramid"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Automation testing is a critical component of modern software development. It involves using software tools to execute pre-scripted tests on a software application before it is released into production. Understanding how to structure these tests efficiently is vital for maintaining a robust CI/CD pipeline."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"What is Automation Testing?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Automation testing replaces manual testing efforts with automated execution. It speeds up regression testing, improves test coverage, and ensures repeatable results. Common frameworks include Selenium for web applications, JUnit for unit testing, and Appium for mobile platforms."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Test Pyramid"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Test Pyramid, conceptualized by Mike Cohn, is a model that guides developers and QA engineers on how to distribute their testing efforts across different granularities. It emphasizes having a solid foundation of fast, low-level tests and fewer, high-level slow tests."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Unit Tests: The base of the pyramid. They test individual functions or components in isolation, are extremely fast, and are written primarily by developers."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Integration Tests: The middle layer. They verify that different modules or services work together correctly, such as database interactions and API endpoints."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"End-to-End (E2E) Tests: The top of the pyramid. They simulate real user journeys through the complete application stack, typically executed via UI automation."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing Your First Automated Unit Test"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Below is an example of a simple unit test written in Python using the pytest framework, demonstrating the foundational layer of the test pyramid."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"def calculate_discount(price, discount_percent):
    if price < 0 or discount_percent < 0:
        raise ValueError("Values cannot be negative")
    return price * (1 - discount_percent / 100)

def test_calculate_discount():
    assert calculate_discount(100, 20) == 80.0
    assert calculate_discount(50, 50) == 25.0"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Automation Testing"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Follow the Test Pyramid ratio: Keep the majority of tests at the unit level to ensure fast feedback loops."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Maintain test independence: Tests should not rely on the state or outcome of other tests."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Integrate into CI/CD: Run your automated test suite automatically on every code commit using tools like GitHub Actions or Jenkins."}]
